      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "To discuss the case of a patient with osteochondritis dissecans, a common disorder primarily affecting children and adolescent patients that involves the medial femoral condyle, and to examine its radiologic appearance."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CLINICAL FEATURES",
          "content": "A 25-year-old man with knee pain sought treatment at a chiropractic clinic. The patient had a 2-year history of left knee pain that was exacerbated by activity and relieved with rest. Plain film radiography of the knee revealed findings consistent with osteochondritis dessicans."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "INTERVENTION AND OUTCOME",
          "content": "The patient was promptly referred for a concurrent orthopedic consultation with subsequent management. A trial basis of conservative care was initiated with the possibility of future surgical intervention if necessary."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "content": "Osteochondritis dissecans is a common disorder of unknown and perhaps controversial cause. The disease is characterized by a fragment of articular cartilage and subchondral bone (osteochondral fragment) that becomes separated from the underlying bone. Treatment is aimed primarily at the preservation of articular surface congruity."
        }
      ]},
